tel 1.0

base bitumen
import soundness

info   tar and ar archives as streams of typed entries: read lazily, built as values, archived from a directory
detail
    _Bitumen_ reads and writes the two sequential archive formats of Unix — **tar**, the
    format a directory tree travels in, and **ar**, the container of every `.deb` package and
    `.a` static library — as streams of typed entries, with none of the mutable
    random-access objects or raw header-and-bytes streams the standard APIs offer. ZIP
    archives are _zeppelin_'s; bitumen shares with it the `archive[Format]` operation, so a
    directory archives into any of the three formats by naming one.

    An archive is data. A `Tarfile` is a `List` of `Tar.Entry`s — a file, a directory, a hard
    or symbolic link, a device, a FIFO or a sparse file, each with its Unix mode, owner and
    timestamp as typed values — and an `Arfile` a `List` of `Ar.Entry` members. Each streams
    as bytes through a `Streamable` given, so writing one to disk is writing any other
    value, and a `Tarfile` compresses with `gzip`, `zlib` or `deflate`. An entry's name is a
    `Tar.Ref`, a `Relative on Tar` path which cannot be absolute or climb above the archive's
    root, so a malicious entry fails to parse rather than escaping on extraction. An entry's
    body is an `Archive.Body`: chunks which pull lazily from the source and memoize, so an
    archive of any size is read entry by entry without being materialised.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val readme = Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])

    val script = Tar.Entry.File
      ( path  = t"bin/run".as[Tar.Ref],
        mode  = UnixMode(ownerExec = true, groupExec = true, otherExec = true),
        user  = UnixUser(1000, t"hamlet"),
        group = UnixGroup(1000, t"hamlet"),
        mtime = 0.bits.u32,
        data  = Archive.Body(t"#!/bin/sh\necho hello\n".in[Data]) )

    val tarball = Tarfile(List(readme, script))
    tarball.gzip                                                   // a .tar.gz byte stream
    Tarfile.read(tarball.source[Data]).map(_.entryName).to[List]   // List(t"README", t"bin/run")
    ```

    Reading runs the other way: `Tarfile.read` and `Arfile.read` parse entries lazily
    straight off a byte stream, absorbing whatever chunk boundaries it arrives in, and a
    compressed archive is a decompressed stream read the same way. A read is single-pass and
    single-owner — consume the entries in order, on one thread — but an entry passed over
    stays readable, because its body memoizes when the iterator advances. Opening an archive
    with `open[Tar]` or `open[Ar]` scopes the source to a block, within which `tar` or `ar`
    is the handle, and `path.create[Tar]()` lends a `TarBuilder` which streams entries of
    unknown length straight to disk.

    A directory tree archives with `directory.archive[Tar]()` or `archive[Ar]()`, through
    galilei's `FilesystemBackend`, in a sorted pre-order which makes two archivings of one
    tree byte-identical; `Archive.Flag.Owner` and `Archive.Flag.Mtime` are the knobs of a
    reproducible build, and `extractTo` is the inverse, unpacking a `Tarfile` onto a
    filesystem.

    The library is split into two source sets:

    - `bitumen.core` — the formats themselves: `Tar`, `Tarfile`, `Ar`, `Arfile`,
      `Archive.Body`, the in-memory `data.open[Tar]()`/`open[Ar]()`, `archive[Tar]`/`[Ar]`
      over any `FilesystemBackend`, `Pax`, `TypeFlag`, `UnixMode`, `UnixUser`, `UnixGroup`
      and `LongNameFormat`. It needs only pure-Scala compression and galilei's
      platform-neutral filesystem abstraction, so it compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and
      WASI alike.
    - `bitumen.jvm` — everything which touches a real file through `java.io`: opening a path
      as an archive (`tarPathOpenable`, `arPathOpenable`), creating one on disk with a
      streaming `TarBuilder` (`tarPathCreatable`), and `extractTo`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The three JVM givens are top-level
    (their subject, a path type, is owned by another library), and are exported by name, so
    `import soundness.*` brings them in; a program importing `bitumen.*` alone must name them
    (`import bitumen.tarPathOpenable`), since a wildcard import does not import givens. The
    symbolic-link policy of `archive[Tar]` is galilei's, chosen by importing
    `filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks` or `filesystemOptions.dereferenceSymlinks` by name.

refer bitumen.Tar
refer bitumen.Tarfile
refer bitumen.Ar
refer bitumen.Arfile
refer bitumen.Archive
refer bitumen.extractTo

entry .Tar   the tar format: a marker type, the plane of entry names, and the home of `Tar.Entry`, `Tar.Error` and `Tar.Handle`
  detail
      `Tar` is an uninhabited sealed trait which names the format wherever a format is chosen
      by type: `path.open[Tar](flags*)`, `data.open[Tar]()`, `path.create[Tar]()` and
      `directory.archive[Tar]()`. Its companion is also the **plane** of entry names — a
      `Tar.Ref` is a `Relative on Tar`, a `/`-separated relative path held to `.Rules` — and
      the home of the format's types: `Tar.Entry` (the kinds of entry), `Tar.Header` (the raw
      512-byte USTAR header), `Tar.Error` and its reasons, `Tar.Flag` (the compression an
      archive is wrapped in) and `Tar.Handle` (the scoped handle of an open archive).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val tarball = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"hello.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"hello".in[Data])))

      tarball.source[Data].memoize.open[Tar]():
        tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)   // List(t"hello.txt")

      tarball.gzip.memoize.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip):
        tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)
      ```

      The companion's givens are in implicit scope, so none needs importing: `.filesystem`
      and `.nominative` make `Relative on Tar` paths decodable from text, `.dataOpenable`
      opens in-memory `Data` as an archive, and `.archivable` archives a directory. Opening a
      *path* on disk as `Tar` needs `bitumen.jvm`'s `tarPathOpenable`.
  refer bitumen.Tarfile
  refer bitumen.Archive
  refer bitumen.tarPathOpenable
  refer bitumen.tar
  entry .Ref   the type of an entry's name: `Relative on Tar`
      A relative path on the `Tar` plane, decoded from text with `t"bin/run".as[Tar.Ref]`
      (or `as[Relative on Tar]`). Being relative, it cannot name an absolute path, and its
      segments are held to `.Rules`, so neither `.` nor `..` can appear: an entry cannot
      escape the directory it is extracted into. A directory entry's name carries no trailing
      `/`; `Tar.Entry#entryName` adds one when the entry is serialized.
  entry .Rules   what a segment of an entry name may be
      A segment must not contain `/`, and must not be `.`, `..` or empty. Text which breaks a
      rule fails to decode with a `Path.Error`; a name read from an archive which breaks one
      raises `Tar.Error` with reason `BadName`.
  entry .compliant   names valid on `Tar` are valid on `Linux`
      The `Linux is Compliant on Tar` given: a `Tar.Ref` may be joined onto a `Path on
      Linux`, which is how `extractTo` places each entry beneath its root.
  entry .compliant2   names valid on `Tar` are valid on `MacOs`
      The `MacOs is Compliant on Tar` given, the macOS counterpart of `.compliant`.
  entry .nominative   the given which holds `Tar` names to `.Rules`
  entry .filesystem   the `Tar` plane as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, with no unique root
      Named `TAR`, with `/` as its separator and `.` and `..` as the self and parent
      segments. `UniqueRoot` is `false`, since entry names are relative. Its separator is
      also what `archive[Tar]` and `archive[Ar]` join a descendant's relative name with.
  entry .dataOpenable   opens in-memory `Data` as a tar archive: `data.open[Tar](flags*)`
      The given which makes `Data` `Openable` in form `Tar`, in implicit scope so that
      `bytes.open[Tar]()` resolves with no import. It needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, and is a `Tar.DataOpenable`. Opening a path on disk is
      `tarPathOpenable`, in `bitumen.jvm`.
  entry .archivable   archives a directory tree as a `Tarfile`: `directory.archive[Tar](flags*)`
      The given which makes a `Path on plane` `Archivable` in form `Tar`, for any plane with a
      `FilesystemBackend on plane` in scope, in implicit scope so that `directory.archive[Tar]()`
      resolves with no import. It also needs a `DereferenceSymlinks` policy (imported from
      `filesystemOptions` by name), a `Tactic[Io.Error]` and a `Tactic[Tar.Error]`, and is a
      `Tar.Archivable[plane]`.
  entry .Archivable   the `Archivable` instance behind `directory.archive[Tar](flags*)`
    detail
        Archives a directory tree as a `Tarfile` through the `FilesystemBackend` of its
        plane, so a tree is archived wherever galilei runs. Every descendant of the root —
        the root itself is not an entry — becomes the `Tar.Entry` of its kind: a file with its
        contents, a directory, a symbolic link with its target (under
        `filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks`; `dereferenceSymlinks` archives what the link
        points at instead), a FIFO, or a character or block device with its numbers. Each
        records the mode, owner and modification time the filesystem reports, unless an
        `Archive.Flag.Owner` or `Archive.Flag.Mtime` replaces them for every entry.

        Entries are visited in pre-order with each directory's children in codepoint order,
        so a directory's entry precedes the entries beneath it, as `tar` expects, and two
        archivings of the same tree produce identical bytes.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks
        import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

        val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"

        val tarball: Tarfile =
          source.archive[Tar](LongNameFormat.Gnu, Archive.Flag.Owner(UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0)))

        tarball.entries.map(_.entryName)   // List(t"a.txt", t"b.txt", t"sub/", t"sub/c.txt")
        ```

        The flags are a `LongNameFormat` — `Pax` unless `Gnu` is given — and any number of
        `Archive.Flag`s. A descendant of a kind tar cannot store (a socket, say) raises
        `Tar.Error` with reason `DeviceCreationUnsupported`; a read which fails raises
        `Io.Error`.
    refer bitumen.Archive
    refer bitumen.Tarfile
    entry #Self   the receiver: a `Path on plane`
    entry #Form   the format selected: `Tar`
    entry #Operand   the flag type: `LongNameFormat | Archive.Flag`
    entry #Result   what the archiving produces: a `Tarfile`
    entry #archive   the entries beneath a root, as a `Tarfile`
        `archive(root, flags)` is what `root.archive[Tar](flags*)` calls: the descendants of
        `root`, in sorted pre-order, with the given flags applied.
  entry .DataOpenable   the `Openable` instance behind `data.open[Tar](flags*)`
    detail
        Opens in-memory `Data` as a tar archive, lending a `Tar.Handle` to the block.
        Archives open read-only: a mode including `Write` is refused with `Tar.Error`, reason
        `WriteUnsupported`. The flags are `Tar.Flag`s, saying which compression wraps the
        bytes, if any.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val bytes: Data = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"a".as[Tar.Ref], t"A".in[Data]))).gzip.memoize

        bytes.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip):
          tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)   // List(t"a")
        ```
    entry #Self   the receiver: `Data`
    entry #Form   the format selected: `Tar`
    entry #Operand   the flag type: `Tar.Flag`
    entry #Result   the handle lent to the block: `Tar.Handle`
    entry #open   parses the data as an archive and runs the block with a `Tar.Handle`
  entry .Header   the 512-byte USTAR header, as raw field slices, with the decoders for its fields
    detail
        A `Tar.Header` holds the sixteen fields of a USTAR header block as undecoded slices
        of `Data` — nothing is interpreted in the case class — and its companion holds the
        decoders the reader applies to them: `.parse` slices a block, `.verifyChecksum`
        checks it, `.decodeOctal` reads a numeric field, `.decodeNulText` a NUL-terminated
        text one, and `.isZeroBlock` recognises the end-of-archive marker. These are the
        reader's own tools, public for a program which must inspect or repair an archive at
        the block level; a program reading entries uses `Tarfile.read`.
    entry .blockSize   the size of a tar block, and of a header: 512 bytes
    entry .checksumOffset   where the checksum field begins in a header: byte 148
    entry .checksumLength   the length of the checksum field: 8 bytes
    entry .parse   slices a 512-byte block into a `Tar.Header`
        A block shorter than `.blockSize` raises `Tar.Error` with reason `TruncatedStream` and
        is then sliced best-effort, with a missing type flag read as a regular file.
    entry .verifyChecksum   checks a header block's recorded checksum against the sum of its bytes
        The sum treats the eight checksum bytes as spaces, as the format prescribes; a
        mismatch raises `Tar.Error` with reason `BadChecksum`, carrying both values. A block
        which fails its checksum cannot be trusted for anything, including the size which
        locates the next header, so the reader ends the walk at that point rather than
        manufacture a cascade of errors from corrupt bytes.
    entry .decodeOctal   a numeric field: optional leading spaces, octal digits, then NUL or space
        Returns the value as a `U32`. A field with no digits, or with a terminator other than
        NUL or space, raises `Tar.Error` with reason `BadOctal`, naming the field.
    entry .decodeNulText   a text field, up to its first NUL byte, as UTF-8
    entry .isZeroBlock   whether a block is entirely zero: the end-of-archive marker
    entry #name   the first 100 bytes of the entry name
    entry #mode   the permission bits, octal
    entry #uid   the owner's numeric id, octal
    entry #gid   the group's numeric id, octal
    entry #size   the entry's size in bytes, octal
    entry #mtime   the modification time in seconds since the epoch, octal
    entry #checksum   the header's checksum, octal
    entry #typeFlag   the one byte which says what kind of entry this is
        The byte a `TypeFlag` encodes to: `'0'` or NUL for a file, `'5'` for a directory,
        `'2'` for a symbolic link, `'x'` for a pax extended header, and so on.
    entry #linkName   the first 100 bytes of a link's target
    entry #magic   the six bytes which should read `ustar` and NUL
    entry #version   the two bytes which should read `00`
    entry #uname   the owner's name, up to 32 bytes
    entry #gname   the group's name, up to 32 bytes
    entry #devMajor   a device entry's major number, octal
    entry #devMinor   a device entry's minor number, octal
    entry #prefix   the USTAR prefix: up to 155 bytes joined before `#name` with `/`
  entry .Entry   an entry of a tar archive: a file, directory, link, device, FIFO, sparse file or metadata block
    detail
        `Tar.Entry` is an enum of the kinds of entry the format stores, each a case class
        carrying the Unix metadata the format exists to preserve: `path` (a `Tar.Ref`), `mode`
        (a `UnixMode`), `user` (a `UnixUser`), `group` (a `UnixGroup`) and `mtime` (seconds
        since the epoch, as a `U32`), and a `pax` map of any extended-header records beyond
        the structural ones (`path`, `linkpath`, `uname` and `gname`, which are folded into
        the fields they override), such as `atime`, `ctime` or a sub-second `mtime`.

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Entry
        row  `File(path, mode, user, group, mtime, data, pax)`  a regular file, its contents an `Archive.Body`
        row  `Directory(path, mode, user, group, mtime, pax)`  a directory; its name serializes with a trailing `/`
        row  `Link(…, target, pax)`  a hard link to `target`, a text naming another entry
        row  `Symlink(…, target, pax)`  a symbolic link to `target`
        row  `CharSpecial(…, device, pax)`  a character device, `device` its `(major, minor)` numbers as `U32`s
        row  `BlockSpecial(…, device, pax)`  a block device, `device` its `(major, minor)` numbers as `U32`s
        row  `Fifo(path, mode, user, group, mtime, pax)`  a named pipe
        row  `Sparse(…, realSize, segments, data, pax)`  a GNU sparse file: `realSize` is its apparent length, `segments` the `SparseSegment`s which hold data, and `data` the concatenation of their bytes
        row  `Pax(records)`  a pax extended header, preceding a real entry
        row  `GnuLong(typeFlag, content)`  a GNU long name or link target, preceding a real entry
        ```

        `Pax` and `GnuLong` are metadata pseudo-entries. `Tarfile` writes them itself from an
        entry's fields, and the reader folds them into the entry which follows, so a program never
        sees one from `Tarfile.read`.

        The companion's `apply` and `at` build the common case, a file, with defaults; the
        case classes take everything explicitly, which is how a mode, an owner or a fixed
        timestamp is set:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val script = Tar.Entry.File
          ( path  = t"bin/run".as[Tar.Ref],
            mode  = UnixMode(ownerExec = true, groupExec = true, otherExec = true),
            user  = UnixUser(1000, t"hamlet"),
            group = UnixGroup(1000, t"hamlet"),
            mtime = 0.bits.u32,
            data  = Archive.Body(t"#!/bin/sh\necho hello\n".in[Data]) )

        val link = Tar.Entry.Symlink
          ( path   = t"bin/start".as[Tar.Ref],
            mode   = UnixMode(),
            user   = UnixUser(0),
            group  = UnixGroup(0),
            mtime  = 0.bits.u32,
            target = t"run" )

        Tarfile(List(script, link)).entries.map(_.typeFlag)   // List(TypeFlag.File, TypeFlag.Symlink)
        ```

        A match over the entries of a read archive distinguishes the kinds:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val tarball = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"hello.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"hello".in[Data])))

        Tarfile.read(tarball.source[Data]).to[List].map:
          case file: Tar.Entry.File       => t"${file.entryName}: ${file.data.size} bytes"
          case link: Tar.Entry.Symlink    => t"${link.entryName} -> ${link.target}"
          case other                      => other.entryName
        ```

        An entry's own methods give its serialized form: `#header` is its 512-byte USTAR
        header, `#dataBlocks` its payload re-blocked into 512-byte blocks, and `#serialize`
        the two together. A name or link target longer than 100 bytes, or an owner or group
        name longer than 32, does not fit the header: `Tarfile` writes the full value in a
        pax header or a GNU long-name block before the entry, according to its
        `LongNameFormat`, and the header field holds the truncated prefix.
    refer bitumen.Archive
    refer bitumen.Tarfile
    refer bitumen.TypeFlag
    entry .apply   a file entry, stamped with the current time
        `Tar.Entry(name, data, mode, user, group)` makes a `File` from anything `Streamable by
        Data` — `Data`, `Text` in a codepage, a `Stream` — read into an `Archive.Body` and
        memoized. The mode defaults to `UnixMode()` (`rw-r--r--`), the owner and group to id
        `0`, and the modification time to the moment of the call. For a fixed timestamp, as a
        reproducible archive needs, use `.at`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])
        ```
    entry .at   a file entry at an explicit modification time, given as any instant type
        As `.apply`, with the timestamp as its third parameter: any type `Abstractable across
        Instants to Long`, such as an aviation `Instant`, or a `Long` of epoch milliseconds
        under `import abstractables.epochMillisecondsAbstractable`. The time is recorded in
        whole seconds. It is a separate method rather than an optional parameter of `.apply`
        because an optional parameter of an inferred type would be left uninferred.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import abstractables.epochMillisecondsAbstractable
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Tar.Entry.at(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data], 86400000L)
        ```
    entry #size   the payload's length in bytes, as recorded in the header
        A file's body length, a `Pax` entry's records, a `GnuLong` entry's content plus its
        NUL, a sparse entry's stored segments; zero for every other kind. Reading a file's
        size drains its `Archive.Body`.
    entry #dataBlocks   the payload as 512-byte blocks, the last zero-padded
        Re-blocks the body's chunks without regard to their boundaries; empty for a kind with
        no payload.
    entry #typeFlag   the `TypeFlag` which names this entry's kind
    entry #entryName   the name as it is written to the header
        The path's text, with a trailing `/` for a directory; `PaxHeaders/0` for a `Pax` entry
        and `././@LongLink` for a `GnuLong` one, as the conventions have it.
    entry #link   a link's target, or `Unset` for an entry which is not a `Link` or `Symlink`
    entry #deviceNumbers   a device's `(major, minor)` numbers, or `Unset` for an entry which is not one
    entry #format   a `U32` as a right-aligned octal header field of a given width
        Pads the octal digits to `width - 1` characters, leaving the last byte for the
        terminator.
    entry #formatLong   a `Long` as a zero-padded octal header field of a given width
        The form used for sparse-map offsets and sizes.
    entry #header   the entry's 512-byte USTAR header block
        Formats every field — the name truncated to 100 bytes, a link target to 100, an
        owner or group name to 32, the size, the mode, the times, the `ustar` magic and
        version, a sparse entry's first four segments and real size — and computes the
        checksum last. The full name or target goes in the preamble `Tarfile` writes.
    entry #serialize   the header and payload blocks, as an `Iterator[Data]^`
        For a sparse entry with more than four segments, the extension blocks come between
        header and payload. The preceding pax or GNU long-name blocks are not included: they
        depend on the archive's `LongNameFormat`, which `Tarfile` knows.
  entry .Error   raised when an archive cannot be read or written, with a `Reason` saying why
    detail
        Error 284: _the TAR archive could not be read or written because `reason`_. Its
        `#reason` is a `Tar.Error.Reason`, each with its own number and message:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `NameTooLong(field, length, maximum)`  1  _the `field` field is `length` bytes, exceeding the USTAR limit of `maximum` bytes_
        row  `BadMagic(actual)`  2  _the USTAR magic bytes are not valid_
        row  `BadChecksum(expected, actual)`  3  _the header checksum did not match_  the reader stops at the corrupt block rather than parse on from untrusted bytes
        row  `UnknownTypeFlag(byte)`  4  _the entry type flag `code` is not recognised_  the entry degrades to a regular file, as POSIX prescribes, never to a directory
        row  `TruncatedStream(needed, got)`  5  _the archive stream ended unexpectedly_  a partial header, a short body, or no terminating zero blocks
        row  `BadOctal(field, data)`  6  _the `field` field did not contain a valid octal value_
        row  `BadPaxRecord(data)`  7  _a PAX extended-header record could not be parsed_
        row  `BadName(text)`  8  _the entry name `text` is not a valid POSIX relative path_  an absolute name, or one with a `.` or `..` segment
        row  `BadSparseMap(text)`  9  _the GNU sparse map `text` could not be parsed_
        row  `DeviceCreationUnsupported(path)`  10  _the special device entry at `path` could not be created on this filesystem_  `extractTo` met a FIFO or device, or `archive[Tar]` met a descendant tar cannot store
        row  `WriteUnsupported`  11  _TAR archives cannot yet be opened for writing_  an `open[Tar]` with a `Write` mode; write with `create[Tar]` or by writing a `Tarfile`
        row  `AlreadyExists`  12  _an archive already exists at this path_  `create[Tar]` without `CreateFlag.Replace`
        row  `CannotWrite(detail)`  13  _the archive could not be written: `detail`_  an I/O failure during `create[Tar]`
        ```

        Every reading API needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]` in scope; `strategies.throwUnsafely`
        throws it, and `safely` or `mitigate` handles it. A read which can recover — a bad
        checksum, an unknown type flag, a malformed pax record — raises rather than aborts,
        so an accruing tactic collects every fault in an archive.
    entry .Reason   why an archive could not be read or written: an enum of thirteen cases
      detail
          Each case is listed on the `Tar.Error` page with its number and message. A
          `Reason` is a `Clarification`, so it reads as the tail of the error's message.
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` to `13`, which becomes the error's sub-number
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   why the archive could not be read or written
  entry .Compression   an empty object reserved for the compression layer; `Tar.Flag` names the compressions
      The object has no members. The compression wrapping an archive is stated with a
      `Tar.Flag` (`Gzip`, `Zlib` or `Deflate`) when it is opened or created, and produced
      with `Tarfile#gzip`, `#zlib` or `#deflate`.
  entry .Flag   the compression an archive is wrapped in: `Gzip`, `Zlib` or `Deflate`
      The flag type of `path.open[Tar](flags*)`, `data.open[Tar](flags*)` and
      `path.create[Tar](flags*)`. Tar has no self-identifying magic for its compression
      layer, so the caller states it — `path.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip)` reads a `.tar.gz` —
      and an archive opened with no flag is read as plain tar. On creation, a flag makes the
      builder compress the finished archive; see `TarBuilder` for what that costs in
      streaming.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val tarball = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"a".as[Tar.Ref], t"A".in[Data])))

      tarball.zlib.memoize.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Zlib):
        tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)
      ```
  entry .Handle   the scoped handle of an open tar archive: `tar` inside an `open[Tar]` block
    detail
        What `path.open[Tar](flags*)` and `data.open[Tar](flags*)` lend to their block, and
        what the contextual accessor `tar` names there. Tar is a sequential format, so
        `#entries` parses lazily from the underlying source, one entry per step, and the
        source stays open only for the block: an entry's payload must be consumed within the
        scope. The handle is an exclusive capability, so neither it nor an iterator or body
        borrowed from it can be returned from the block.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

        val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "release.tar.gz"

        path.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip):
          tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)
        ```

        The iterator is single-pass: an entry passed over stays readable, since its body
        memoizes as the iterator advances, but the sequence itself is not replayable within
        the scope. To keep the entries, convert them with `to[List]` inside the block; the
        bodies of the entries kept are buffered as the read moves past them.
    refer bitumen.tar
    refer bitumen.tarPathOpenable
    entry #entries   the archive's entries, parsed lazily as the iterator advances
        An `Iterator[Tar.Entry]` which captures the handle, so it cannot outlive the block.
        Advancing it drains whatever of the previous entry's body was not read into that
        entry's `Archive.Body`, so the cursor stands at the next header. A malformed archive
        raises `Tar.Error` as the fault is reached.

entry .SparseSegment   one data-holding run of a GNU sparse file: its offset and length
  detail
      A `Tar.Entry.Sparse` records which runs of its apparent `realSize` hold data as a list
      of `SparseSegment(offset, length)`; the bytes of those runs, concatenated, are the
      entry's `data`. The first four segments are written in the header and the rest in
      extension blocks, which the reader reassembles.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val sparse = Tar.Entry.Sparse
        ( path     = t"sparse.bin".as[Tar.Ref],
          mode     = UnixMode(),
          user     = UnixUser(0),
          group    = UnixGroup(0),
          mtime    = 0.bits.u32,
          realSize = 10000L,
          segments = List(SparseSegment(0L, 100L), SparseSegment(5000L, 200L)),
          data     = Archive.Body(Array.fill[Byte](300)('X'.toByte)) )

      Tarfile.read(Tarfile(List(sparse)).source[Data]).to[List].map:
        case entry: Tar.Entry.Sparse => entry.segments.size   // 2
        case _                       => 0
      ```
  entry #offset   where the run begins in the file's apparent extent
  entry #length   how many bytes the run holds

entry .Tarfile   a tar archive as a value: a list of entries which streams as blocks, plain or compressed
  detail
      `Tarfile(entries, longNameFormat)` is the in-memory form of an archive: its
      `#entries`, in order, and the convention for names too long for a USTAR header (`Pax`
      unless `LongNameFormat.Gnu` is given). It is `Streamable by Data` (`.streamable`), so
      it is written to a file, sent over a connection or hashed as any other value, with the
      two terminating zero blocks included; and `#gzip`, `#zlib` and `#deflate` are the
      compressed streams the format usually travels in. Nothing is assembled in memory: each
      entry's blocks are produced as the stream is consumed, and an entry's body is opened
      only when it is reached.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A tarball built from entries
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val readme = Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])
            val tarball = Tarfile(List(readme))
      frame
        label Its bytes, as tar blocks
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val readme = Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])
            val tarball = Tarfile(List(readme))
            tarball.source[Data].memoize.length   // 2048: a header, a data block, two zero blocks
      frame
        label Compressed, as a .tar.gz stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val readme = Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])
            val tarball = Tarfile(List(readme))
            val gzipped: Data = tarball.gzip.memoize
      frame
        label Read back from the compressed stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val readme = Tar.Entry(t"README".as[Tar.Ref], t"Read me first".in[Data])
            val tarball = Tarfile(List(readme))
            val gzipped: Data = tarball.gzip.memoize
            Tarfile.read(gzipped.stream.decompress[Gzip]).map(_.entryName).to[List]   // List(t"README")
      ##
      ```

      Reading runs the other way: `.read` parses entries lazily off any byte stream, and
      `.from` reads a whole archive back into a `Tarfile`, which re-serializes with the same
      entries. A `Tarfile` comes from `directory.archive[Tar]()` too, and unpacks onto a
      filesystem with `extractTo` (in `bitumen.jvm`). Tar permits duplicate names, later
      entries superseding earlier ones on extraction, so nothing is checked when a list is
      built.
  refer bitumen.Tar
  refer bitumen.LongNameFormat
  refer bitumen.extractTo
  refer bitumen.TarBuilder
  entry .zeroBlock   a block of 512 zero bytes: two of them end every archive
  entry .streamable   streams a `Tarfile` as its tar blocks: `tarfile.source[Data]`, `path.write(tarfile)`
      The given which makes a `Tarfile` `Streamable by Data over Credit`, in implicit scope.
      The stream is every entry's preamble (pax or GNU long-name blocks, as the
      `longNameFormat` requires), header and padded payload, then two zero blocks; each
      entry's body is opened when the stream reaches it.
  entry .read   parses a byte stream as tar entries, lazily, one entry per step
      `Tarfile.read(stream)` returns an `Iterator[Tar.Entry]^` which parses straight off the
      stream as it advances, absorbing whatever chunk boundaries the stream arrives in: the
      archive is never materialised. It consumes the stream — the stream's ownership passes
      to the iterator — and needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]`. A compressed archive is a
      decompressed stream read the same way: `Tarfile.read(stream.decompress[Gzip])`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val tarball = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"hello.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"hello".in[Data])))

      Tarfile.read(tarball.source[Data]).to[List].map:
        case file: Tar.Entry.File => file.data.memoize.utf8   // t"hello"
        case other                => other.entryName
      ```

      The entries are single-owner: consume them in order, on one thread. A file entry's
      `Archive.Body` pulls off the shared cursor in bounded chunks as it is read, so an
      in-order consumer holds memory bounded by the entries it retains; advancing to the
      next entry first drains what remains of the previous body into its memo, so an entry
      passed over is still readable, at the cost of buffering it. Pax extended headers and
      GNU long-name blocks are folded into the entry they precede (a `path`, `linkpath`,
      `uname` or `gname` record overrides the header field; every other record lands in the
      entry's `pax` map), and a sparse file's extension blocks are reassembled. The walk ends
      at the first zero block; a stream which ends without one raises `Tar.Error` with
      reason `TruncatedStream`, and a block which fails its checksum ends the walk with
      `BadChecksum`.
  entry .from   reads a whole byte stream back into a `Tarfile`
      `Tarfile.from(stream)` is `.read` with the entries collected into a `List`: a
      `Tarfile` which re-serializes with the same entries and the default `LongNameFormat`.
      It consumes the stream and needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]`. The bodies are memoized, so
      the whole archive is held in memory; for one too large for that, iterate `.read`.
  entry #entries   the archive's entries, in the order they are written
  entry #longNameFormat   how a name or link target over 100 bytes is written: `Pax` or `Gnu`
  entry #gzip   the archive compressed as gzip: a `.tar.gz` stream
      A `Stream[Data] over Credit` which compresses the blocks as it is consumed; read it
      back with `Tarfile.read(stream.decompress[Gzip])`, or open its bytes with
      `open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip)`.
  entry #zlib   the archive compressed in zlib format
      Read back with `decompress[Zlib]` or `Tar.Flag.Zlib`.
  entry #deflate   the archive as a raw deflate stream, with no gzip or zlib framing
      Read back with `decompress[Deflate]` or `Tar.Flag.Deflate`.

entry .LongNameFormat   how a tar name longer than a USTAR header holds is written: `Pax` or `Gnu`
    A USTAR header holds 100 bytes of name and 100 of link target, 32 of owner and group
    name. A value which does not fit is written in full in a block before the entry: a POSIX
    pax extended header (`Pax`, the default, which every modern `tar` reads, and which also
    carries the `pax` records of an entry) or GNU's `././@LongLink` pseudo-entries (`Gnu`,
    for an archive bound for an older GNU `tar`). Under `Gnu`, an entry's non-structural pax
    records still go in a pax header, and only `path` and `linkpath` take the GNU form. The
    format is chosen when an archive is built — `Tarfile(entries, LongNameFormat.Gnu)`,
    `directory.archive[Tar](LongNameFormat.Gnu)`, `path.create[Tar](LongNameFormat.Gnu)` —
    and the reader accepts either.

entry .Archive   what the sequential formats share: the memoizing entry body and the archiving flags
  detail
      `Archive` holds the two things common to `Tar` and `Ar` (and to zeppelin's `Zip`, for
      the flags): `Archive.Body`, the replayable, lazily-pulled body of an entry, which both
      readers lend to their consumers and both writers stream from; and `Archive.Flag`, the
      flags of `directory.archive[Format](flags*)` which make an archive reproducible by
      replacing the owner and timestamp the filesystem reports.
  refer bitumen.Tar
  refer bitumen.Ar
  entry .Flag   a flag of `directory.archive[Format](flags*)`: a fixed owner or a fixed timestamp
    detail
        The knobs of a reproducible build, as `tar --owner`, `--group` and `--mtime` are.
        Each replaces what the filesystem reports for *every* entry of the archive:

        - `Owner(user, group)` — every entry records this `UnixUser` and `UnixGroup`, in
          place of the file's owner.
        - `Mtime(seconds)` — every entry records this modification time, in seconds since
          the epoch, in place of the file's. `Archive.Flag.Mtime(instant)` takes any instant
          type instead.

        They apply to `archive[Tar]`, `archive[Ar]` and `archive[Zip]` alike, mixed with the
        format's own flags:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks
        import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

        val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"
        source.archive[Tar](Archive.Flag.Owner(UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0)), Archive.Flag.Mtime(0L))
        ```
    entry .Mtime   the companion of the `Mtime` flag, which also takes an instant
      entry .apply   `Archive.Flag.Mtime(instant)`: a fixed timestamp from any instant type
          Takes any value `Abstractable across Instants to Long` — an aviation `Instant`, or
          epoch milliseconds under `import abstractables.epochMillisecondsAbstractable` —
          and records it in whole seconds. `Mtime(seconds: Long)`, the case itself, takes the
          seconds directly.
  entry .Body   the replayable body of an entry: chunks pulled lazily from the source and memoized
    detail
        An `Archive.Body` is what a `Tar.Entry.File`, `Tar.Entry.Sparse` or `Ar.Entry` holds
        as its contents. Built with `Archive.Body(chunks*)` from data in hand, it is simply
        those chunks; lent by `Tarfile.read` or `Arfile.read`, it pulls its chunks from the
        reader's cursor in bounded pieces as they are first read, and memoizes them, so the
        region of the source is read exactly once however many consumers stream it, and
        each `#stream` replays from the first chunk. An in-order consumer of a streaming read
        therefore holds memory bounded by the entries it keeps: a body's chunks are
        reclaimed with its entry.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val body = Archive.Body(t"hello, ".in[Data], t"world".in[Data])
        body.size             // 12
        body.memoize.utf8     // t"hello, world"
        body.chunks.to[List].size   // 2
        ```

        A lent body is valid while its reader's source is: within an `open[Tar]` block, or
        before the stream `Tarfile.read` was given is closed. Read it, or `#memoize` it, there.
    refer bitumen.Tar
    refer bitumen.Ar
    entry .apply   an in-memory body of the given chunks
        `Archive.Body(chunks*)` takes any number of `Data` values, drops any that are empty,
        and pulls nothing lazily. `Archive.Body()` is an empty body.
    entry .empty   the empty body: `Archive.Body()`
    entry #size   the body's length in bytes
        Drains the body from its producer first, if it was lent, so the whole of it is
        memoized.
    entry #chunks   the body's chunks, replayed from the start, as an `Iterator[Data]^`
        Memoized chunks are replayed; chunks not yet read pull from the producer as the
        iterator advances. The iterator captures the body.
    entry #stream   a fresh `Stream[Data] over Credit` over the chunks, from the start
        What `Tar.Entry` is built from and what a consumer reads with `read[Text]` or
        `decompress[Gzip]`; each call starts again at the first chunk.
    entry #memoize   the whole body as one `Data` value
        Drains the body and concatenates its chunks; a body of a single chunk returns it as
        is. For an entry of a compressed archive this is the entry's own bytes, not the
        archive's.

entry .tar   the open archive's handle inside an `open[Tar]` block
    A transparent inline accessor for the `Tar.Handle` the block was given, in the manner of
    galilei's `file`: `tar.entries` iterates the archive. It keeps the handle's precise type,
    so what the block may do is what it was opened for. Under `import soundness.*`, it is
    the umbrella's `tar`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val bytes: Data = Tarfile(List(Tar.Entry(t"a".as[Tar.Ref], t"A".in[Data]))).source[Data].memoize

    bytes.open[Tar]():
      tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)   // List(t"a")
    ```

entry .ar   the open archive's handle inside an `open[Ar]` block
    A transparent inline accessor for the `Ar.Handle` the block was given: `ar.entries`
    iterates the archive's members. It keeps the handle's precise type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val member = Ar.Entry(t"debian-binary", Archive.Body(t"2.0\n".in[Data]), 0L, UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0), 33188)
    val bytes: Data = Arfile(List(member)).source[Data].memoize

    bytes.open[Ar]():
      ar.entries.to[List].map(_.name)   // List(t"debian-binary")
    ```

entry .Ar   the ar format: a marker type and the home of `Ar.Entry`, `Ar.Error`, `Ar.Header` and `Ar.Handle`
  detail
      `ar` is the container of every `.deb` package and every `.a` static library: an
      eight-byte magic, then a flat run of members, each a sixty-byte header followed inline
      by its payload, padded to an even offset. It carries no index, no checksum, no entry
      type and — unlike tar — no end-of-archive marker, so a member is just a name, its Unix
      metadata and a body, the archive ends when the stream does, and a member whose
      recorded size overruns the file is the only corruption the format can signal.

      `Ar` is an uninhabited sealed trait which names the format wherever one is chosen by
      type: `path.open[Ar]()`, `data.open[Ar]()` and `directory.archive[Ar]()`. Its companion
      holds the format's constants and types: `Ar.Entry` (a member), `Ar.Header` (the raw
      header and its decoders), `Ar.Role` (what a GNU member is for), `Ar.Error` and
      `Ar.Handle`. There are no open flags, because a `.deb` is plain `ar`: it is the members
      inside which are compressed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val control =
        Ar.Entry(t"control.tar.xz", Archive.Body(t"...".in[Data]), 0L, UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0), 33188)

      val deb = Arfile(List(control))
      Arfile.read(deb.source[Data]).map(_.name).to[List]   // List(t"control.tar.xz")

      deb.source[Data].memoize.open[Ar]():
        ar.entries.to[List].map(_.name)
      ```

      The companion's givens are in implicit scope: `.dataOpenable` opens in-memory `Data`
      and `.archivable` archives a directory. Opening a *path* on disk as `Ar` needs
      `bitumen.jvm`'s `arPathOpenable`.
  refer bitumen.Arfile
  refer bitumen.Archive
  refer bitumen.arPathOpenable
  refer bitumen.ar
  entry .magic   the eight bytes which begin every archive: `!<arch>` and a newline
  entry .magicSize   the length of the magic: 8 bytes
  entry .headerSize   the length of a member header: 60 bytes
  entry .dataOpenable   opens in-memory `Data` as an ar archive: `data.open[Ar]()`
      The given which makes `Data` `Openable` in form `Ar`, in implicit scope so that
      `bytes.open[Ar]()` resolves with no import. It needs a `Tactic[Ar.Error]`, and is an
      `Ar.DataOpenable`. Opening a path on disk is `arPathOpenable`, in `bitumen.jvm`.
  entry .archivable   archives a directory tree as an `Arfile`: `directory.archive[Ar](flags*)`
      The given which makes a `Path on plane` `Archivable` in form `Ar`, for any plane with a
      `FilesystemBackend on plane` in scope, in implicit scope so that `directory.archive[Ar]()`
      resolves with no import. It needs a `Tactic[Io.Error]` and a `Tactic[Ar.Error]`, and is
      an `Ar.Archivable[plane]`.
  entry .Archivable   the `Archivable` instance behind `directory.archive[Ar](flags*)`
    detail
        Archives a directory tree as a flat `Arfile` through the `FilesystemBackend` of its
        plane. `ar` has no directory entries, so the tree is flattened: every regular file
        beneath the root becomes a member named by its slash-joined relative path (a GNU
        long name, past fifteen characters), directories contribute nothing, and symbolic
        links are followed, since a link cannot be a member either. A FIFO or device aborts
        with `Ar.Error`, reason `Unrepresentable`. Each member's `mode` is the whole
        `st_mode`, type bits included, as `ar` stores it, and `Archive.Flag.Owner` and
        `Archive.Flag.Mtime` replace the owner and timestamp the filesystem reports. The
        walk is the same sorted pre-order as `archive[Tar]`, so the archive is reproducible.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

        val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"
        source.archive[Ar]().entries.map(_.name)   // List(t"a.txt", t"b.txt", t"sub/c.txt")
        ```
    refer bitumen.Archive
    refer bitumen.Arfile
    entry #Self   the receiver: a `Path on plane`
    entry #Form   the format selected: `Ar`
    entry #Operand   the flag type: `Archive.Flag`
    entry #Result   what the archiving produces: an `Arfile`
    entry #archive   the regular files beneath a root, as an `Arfile`
  entry .Role   what a GNU archive member is: `Regular`, `SymbolTable` or `NameTable`
      `ar` has no type field, so the kinds of member are told apart by name alone. A
      `.deb` holds `Regular` members only; the other two appear in `.a` static libraries,
      which nest inside the `data.tar` of every `-dev` package. `SymbolTable` is the member
      named `/` (or `/SYM64/`, the 64-bit table GNU writes when offsets outgrow 32 bits) and
      `NameTable` the `//` member which holds every name longer than fifteen characters.
      `Ar.Entry#role` classifies a member, so a listing can skip the tables:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val member = Ar.Entry(t"a.txt", Archive.Body(t"aa".in[Data]), 0L, UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0), 33188)

      Arfile.read(Arfile(List(member)).source[Data]).to[List]
      . filter(_.role == Ar.Role.Regular)
      . map(_.name)
      ```
  entry .Error   raised when an ar archive cannot be read or written, with a `Reason` saying why
    detail
        Error 286: _the ar archive could not be read or written because `reason`_. Its
        `#reason` is an `Ar.Error.Reason`, each with its own number and message:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `BadMagic(actual)`  1  _the archive does not begin with the ar magic bytes_
        row  `BadTerminator(actual)`  2  _the header is not terminated by the two magic bytes_  the cursor is no longer on a header boundary, the only structural check a header offers
        row  `BadDecimal(field, data)`  3  _the `field` field did not contain a valid decimal value_
        row  `BadOctal(field, data)`  4  _the `field` field did not contain a valid octal value_  the `mode` field is octal; every other numeric field is decimal
        row  `TruncatedStream(needed, got)`  5  _the archive stream ended unexpectedly_  a short header, or a body shorter than its header said
        row  `BadLongNameRef(reference)`  6  _the long-name reference `reference` does not resolve in the archive's name table_  a `/`-prefixed name with no `//` member before it, or an offset outside the table
        row  `UnsupportedNameFormat(name)`  7  _the member name `name` uses the BSD length-prefixed form, which is not supported_  BSD `ar`'s `#1/<length>` form, which does not occur in Debian archives
        row  `WriteUnsupported`  8  _ar archives cannot yet be opened for writing_  an `open[Ar]` with a `Write` mode; write by writing an `Arfile`
        row  `Unrepresentable(name)`  9  _the entry `name` is neither a regular file nor a directory, which ar cannot store_  `archive[Ar]` met a FIFO or device
        ```

        Every reading API needs a `Tactic[Ar.Error]` in scope. Because the format has no
        checksum and no end marker, a short header cannot be parsed best-effort: the
        recorded size is the only thing locating the next header, so a truncation aborts.
    entry .Reason   why an ar archive could not be read or written: an enum of nine cases
      detail
          Each case is listed on the `Ar.Error` page with its number and message. A `Reason`
          is a `Clarification`, so it reads as the tail of the error's message.
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` to `9`, which becomes the error's sub-number
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   why the archive could not be read or written
  entry .Entry   one member of an ar archive: its name, Unix metadata and body
    detail
        A member is a `name`, a modification time, an owner and group, a `mode` and an
        `Archive.Body`. `Ar.Entry(name, data, mtime, user, group, mode)` builds one for
        writing: the stored name takes the SysV terminating `/`, which is what lets a name
        with trailing spaces survive a round trip. A member read from an archive carries two
        more things: `#rawName`, the literal sixteen-byte field (a GNU long-name reference
        such as `/19`, or a name with its `/`), and `#origin`, the verbatim header, so that
        the archive re-serializes byte for byte. `#name` is always the resolved name: a long
        name looked up in the `//` table, a SysV `/` stripped.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val control =
          Ar.Entry(t"control.tar.xz", Archive.Body(t"...".in[Data]), 1716989111L, UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0), 33188)

        control.role         // Ar.Role.Regular
        control.size         // 3
        control.rawName      // t"control.tar.xz/"
        ```

        The `mode` is the raw octal field — `33188`, which is `100644` — rather than a
        `UnixMode`, because `ar` stores the file-type bits alongside the permissions, which
        `UnixMode` does not represent, and dropping them would change the formatted header.
    refer bitumen.Arfile
    refer bitumen.Archive
    entry .padded   a payload's size rounded up to even: where the next header begins
        A member whose size is odd is followed by one newline byte, so every header starts
        at an even offset.
    entry .apply   a member built for writing: `Ar.Entry(name, data, mtime, user, group, mode)`
        The stored `rawName` is `name` with a terminating `/`, in the SysV manner; no verbatim
        header is retained, so the header is formatted from the fields. `mtime` is seconds
        since the epoch; `mode` is the whole `st_mode`, `33188` (`100644`) for an ordinary
        file.
    entry #name   the member's name, resolved: a long name looked up, a trailing `/` stripped
    entry #rawName   the literal sixteen-byte name field, as written in the archive
        `/`, `//` or `/SYM64/` for the tables, `/<offset>` for a GNU long-name reference, or
        the name with its SysV terminating `/`.
    entry #mtime   the modification time, in seconds since the epoch
    entry #user   the owner, as a `UnixUser` (by id only: `ar` stores no names)
    entry #group   the group, as a `UnixGroup`
    entry #mode   the whole `st_mode`, type bits included, as `ar` stores it
    entry #data   the member's body, an `Archive.Body`
    entry #origin   the verbatim header this member was parsed with, or `Unset` for one built in code
    entry #role   whether the member is `Regular`, the `SymbolTable` or the `NameTable`
        Decided by `#rawName`: `/` and `/SYM64/` are the symbol table, `//` the name table,
        and anything else a regular member.
    entry #size   the body's length in bytes
    entry #header   the sixty-byte header: the verbatim one if parsed, else one formatted from the fields
        Re-emitting the original matters for byte-exact reconstruction: GNU writes the `//`
        name table with its `mtime`, `uid`, `gid` and `mode` fields left blank, and no
        decoded number can tell blank from zero. A formatted header writes `#rawName`, not
        the resolved name, so a long-name archive still round-trips against its own table.
    entry #serialize   the header, the body's chunks, and the pad byte if the size is odd
  entry .Header   the sixty-byte member header, as raw field slices, with the decoders for its fields
    detail
        An `Ar.Header` holds the seven fields of a member header as undecoded slices of
        `Data`, as `Tar.Header` does for tar. Every field is left-aligned ASCII padded with
        trailing spaces — the opposite of tar's right-aligned, space-led form — and every
        numeric field is decimal except `mode`, which is octal. The companion holds the
        reader's decoders and the writer's `.format`, public for a program which must
        inspect or repair an archive at the header level.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Ar.Header.decodeDecimal(t"1716989111  ".in[Data], t"mtime")   // 1716989111L
        Ar.Header.decodeOctal(t"100644  ".in[Data], t"mode")          // 33188L
        Ar.Header.decodeName(t"SAX.o/          ".in[Data])            // t"SAX.o/"
        Ar.Header.format(t"a.txt/", 1716989111L, 0, 0, 33188, 3L).length   // 60
        ```
    entry .headerSize   the length of a header: 60 bytes
    entry .terminator   the two bytes which end every header: a backquote and a newline
    entry .padByte   the newline which follows an odd-sized payload
    entry .parse   slices a sixty-byte block into an `Ar.Header`
        A block shorter than `.headerSize` aborts with `Ar.Error`, reason `TruncatedStream`:
        with no checksum and no end marker, a short header cannot be parsed best-effort.
    entry .verifyTerminator   checks that a header ends in `.terminator`
        A mismatch aborts with `Ar.Error`, reason `BadTerminator`: the cursor is no longer on
        a header boundary.
    entry .decodeDecimal   a decimal field: digits, then space or NUL padding
        An entirely blank field decodes as zero, since some archivers leave `uid` and `gid`
        empty; a non-digit, or a digit resuming after padding, raises `Ar.Error` with reason
        `BadDecimal`, naming the field.
    entry .decodeOctal   an octal field, as `.decodeDecimal` reads a decimal one
        For the `mode` field; a malformed one raises `Ar.Error` with reason `BadOctal`.
    entry .decodeName   the name field with its trailing space padding removed
        The SysV terminating `/` is kept: `/` and `//` name the symbol and name tables, and
        telling those apart from a terminated name is the reader's job, which needs the raw
        form. `Ar.Entry#name` is the resolved name.
    entry .format   a header from its fields, every one left-aligned and space-padded
        `format(name, mtime, user, group, mode, size)`: a value too wide for its field is
        clipped. The name is written as given, so pass the raw form with its `/`.
    entry #name   the sixteen-byte name field
    entry #mtime   the twelve-byte modification time, decimal
    entry #uid   the six-byte owner id, decimal
    entry #gid   the six-byte group id, decimal
    entry #mode   the eight-byte mode, octal
    entry #size   the ten-byte payload size, decimal
    entry #terminator   the two terminating bytes
  entry .DataOpenable   the `Openable` instance behind `data.open[Ar]()`
    detail
        Opens in-memory `Data` as an ar archive, lending an `Ar.Handle` to the block.
        Archives open read-only: a mode including `Write` is refused with `Ar.Error`, reason
        `WriteUnsupported`. The operand type is `Nothing`: there are no flags, since a `.deb`
        is plain `ar`.
    entry #Self   the receiver: `Data`
    entry #Form   the format selected: `Ar`
    entry #Operand   the flag type: `Nothing`, as there are no flags
    entry #Result   the handle lent to the block: `Ar.Handle`
    entry #open   parses the data as an archive and runs the block with an `Ar.Handle`
  entry .Handle   the scoped handle of an open ar archive: `ar` inside an `open[Ar]` block
    detail
        What `path.open[Ar]()` and `data.open[Ar]()` lend to their block, and what the
        contextual accessor `ar` names there. `ar` is a sequential format, so `#entries`
        parses lazily from the underlying source, one member per step, and the source stays
        open only for the block: a member's payload must be consumed within the scope. The
        handle is an exclusive capability, so neither it nor anything borrowed from it can
        be returned from the block.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

        val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "hello.deb"

        path.open[Ar]():
          ar.entries.to[List].map(_.name)   // List(t"debian-binary", t"control.tar.xz", t"data.tar.xz")
        ```

        The iterator is single-pass: a member passed over stays readable, since its body
        memoizes as the iterator advances, but the sequence is not replayable within the
        scope.
    refer bitumen.ar
    refer bitumen.arPathOpenable
    entry #entries   the archive's members, parsed lazily as the iterator advances
        An `Iterator[Ar.Entry]` which captures the handle, so it cannot outlive the block. A
        malformed archive raises `Ar.Error` as the fault is reached.

entry .Arfile   an ar archive as a value: a list of members which streams as bytes
  detail
      `Arfile(entries)` is the in-memory form of an `ar` archive: its `#entries`, in order.
      It is `Streamable by Data` (`.streamable`), so writing it to a file is writing any
      other value: the magic, then each member's header, padded payload and nothing more,
      since the format has no trailing marker. `.read` parses members lazily off a byte
      stream and `.from` reads a whole archive back, which re-serializes byte for byte.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def member(name: Text, content: Text): Ar.Entry =
        Ar.Entry(name, Archive.Body(content.in[Data]), 1716989111L, UnixUser(0), UnixGroup(0), 33188)

      val deb = Arfile(List(member(t"debian-binary", t"2.0\n"), member(t"control.tar", t"cc")))
      val bytes: Data = deb.source[Data].memoize

      Arfile.read(bytes.stream).map(_.name).to[List]        // List(t"debian-binary", t"control.tar")
      Arfile.from(bytes.stream).source[Data].memoize.to[List] == bytes.to[List]   // byte for byte
      ```
  refer bitumen.Ar
  refer bitumen.Archive
  entry .streamable   streams an `Arfile` as its bytes: `arfile.source[Data]`, `path.write(arfile)`
      The given which makes an `Arfile` `Streamable by Data over Credit`, in implicit scope.
      Each member's body is opened when the stream reaches it.
  entry .read   parses a byte stream as ar members, lazily, one member per step
      `Arfile.read(stream)` returns an `Iterator[Ar.Entry]^` which parses straight off the
      stream as it advances, absorbing whatever chunk boundaries it arrives in. It consumes
      the stream and needs a `Tactic[Ar.Error]`. The members are single-owner — consume
      them in order, on one thread — and each body pulls off the shared cursor in bounded
      chunks, memoizing if the iterator moves past it, as `Tarfile.read` does. The one
      member read whole is the `//` name table, since every later long name resolves
      against it. The archive ends when the stream does; a stream which does not begin with
      `Ar.magic` aborts with `BadMagic`.
  entry .from   reads a whole byte stream back into an `Arfile`
      `Arfile.from(stream)` is `.read` with the members collected into a `List`. Because
      each member keeps its verbatim header and raw name, the result re-serializes byte for
      byte: `Arfile.from(stream).source[Data]` reproduces the original.
  entry #entries   the archive's members, in order

entry .Pax   the POSIX pax extended-header record format: `length key=value` lines
  detail
      A pax extended header is a payload of records, each a decimal length (which counts
      itself), a space, a key, `=`, a value and a newline. `Tarfile` writes one before any
      entry whose name, link target, owner or group name overflows the USTAR header, or
      which carries `pax` records of its own; the reader parses it and folds it into the
      entry which follows. These functions are the encoder and decoder, public for a program
      which handles the records itself.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Pax.record(t"path", t"a/very/long/name").utf8   // t"25 path=a/very/long/name\n"
      Pax.parse(Pax.records(List(t"atime" -> t"1700000000.5", t"comment" -> t"test")))
      ```
  refer bitumen.Tar
  refer bitumen.LongNameFormat
  entry .record   one record, `length key=value` and a newline, as bytes
      The length is the whole record's, its own digits included, which the encoder solves
      for.
  entry .records   several records concatenated, as the payload of a pax entry
  entry .parse   the records of a pax payload, as a `Map` of key to value
      Needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]`. A record with a malformed length prefix raises `Tar.Error`
      with reason `BadPaxRecord` and ends the scan, since the cursor can no longer be
      trusted; a record with no `=` raises the same and skips just that record, so every
      malformed record accrues under an accruing tactic.

entry .TypeFlag   the kind of a tar entry, as the one-byte flag which names it in the header
  detail
      Every `Tar.Entry` has a `typeFlag`: `File` (`'0'`), `Link` (`'1'`), `Symlink` (`'2'`),
      `CharSpecial` (`'3'`), `BlockSpecial` (`'4'`), `Directory` (`'5'`), `Fifo` (`'6'`),
      `Contiguous` (`'7'`, read as a regular file), `NextFile` (`'x'`, a pax header for the next
      entry), `GlobalExtension` (`'g'`, a pax header for every following entry), `LongLink`
      (`'K'`) and `LongName` (`'L'`, the GNU long-name blocks) and `Sparse` (`'S'`, a GNU sparse
      file). `#id` is the byte, as a `Char`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      TypeFlag.Directory.id   // '5'
      ```
  entry #id   the byte written at offset 156 of the header, as a `Char`

entry .UnixMode   a Unix permission mode as twelve named bits
  detail
      The permission bits of a tar entry: `setUid`, `setGid`, and read, write and execute for
      owner, group and other, each a `Boolean`. `UnixMode()` is `rw-r--r--` (`0644`): the
      owner may read and write, everyone else may read. `.from` decodes an integer mode and
      `#int` encodes one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val executable = UnixMode(ownerExec = true, groupExec = true, otherExec = true)
      executable.int             // 493, which is 0755
      UnixMode.from(493) == executable
      ```

      `Ar.Entry#mode` is not a `UnixMode` but the whole `st_mode` as an `Int`, since `ar`
      stores the file-type bits too.
  refer bitumen.Tar
  entry .from   a mode from its integer bits: `UnixMode.from(493)` is `rwxr-xr-x`
      Only the low twelve bits are read; file-type bits above them are ignored.
  entry #setUid   the set-user-id bit, `04000`
  entry #setGid   the set-group-id bit, `02000`
  entry #ownerRead   the owner may read, `0400`; `true` by default
  entry #ownerWrite   the owner may write, `0200`; `true` by default
  entry #ownerExec   the owner may execute, `0100`
  entry #groupRead   the group may read, `040`; `true` by default
  entry #groupWrite   the group may write, `020`
  entry #groupExec   the group may execute, `010`
  entry #otherRead   anyone may read, `04`; `true` by default
  entry #otherWrite   anyone may write, `02`
  entry #otherExec   anyone may execute, `01`
  entry #int   the mode as an integer: the sum of its bits

entry .UnixUser   the owner of an entry: a numeric id and, if known, a name
  detail
      `UnixUser(1000, t"hamlet")` or `UnixUser(0)`. Tar stores both, the name in the 32-byte
      `uname` field or a pax `uname` record when longer; `ar` stores the id alone. A name read
      from an archive which is blank is `Unset`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      UnixUser(1000, t"hamlet").name   // t"hamlet"
      UnixUser(0).name                // Unset
      ```
  entry #value   the numeric user id
  entry #name   the user's name, or `Unset` if none is recorded

entry .UnixGroup   the group of an entry: a numeric id and, if known, a name
  detail
      `UnixGroup(1000, t"staff")` or `UnixGroup(0)`, the counterpart of `UnixUser`. Tar stores
      the name in the 32-byte `gname` field or a pax `gname` record when longer; `ar` stores
      the id alone.
  entry #value   the numeric group id
  entry #name   the group's name, or `Unset` if none is recorded

entry .TarBuilder   the authoring handle of `path.create[Tar](flags*)`: entries stream to disk as they are inserted
  detail
      A JVM-only class, in `bitumen.jvm`. `path.create[Tar](flags*)` (through
      `tarPathCreatable`) lends a `TarBuilder` to its block; the block inserts entries, and
      when it returns the archive is complete on disk. The builder is the only way to write
      a tar entry of *unknown* length end to end: on an uncompressed target it writes
      straight through to a hidden temporary sibling as entries are inserted, a file whose
      payload is lazy streaming to disk in bounded chunks, its header written as a
      placeholder and backpatched with the real size and checksum once the body has passed.
      A compressed target (a `Tar.Flag` among the flags) cannot be backpatched through the
      compressor, so entries accumulate and serialize when the scope closes, each payload
      buffered. Either way the target appears atomically, by a rename, or not at all: an
      exception escaping the block leaves nothing behind.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

      val target: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "made.tar"

      target.create[Tar](CreateFlag.Replace): builder ?=>
        builder.insert(t"first.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"one".in[Data])
        builder.file(t"log.bin".as[Tar.Ref]): entry ?=>
          for i <- 1 to 5 do entry.put(Array.fill[Byte](1000)(i.toByte))
        builder.insert(t"third.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"three".in[Data])

      target.open[Tar]():
        tar.entries.to[List].map(_.entryName)   // List(t"first.txt", t"log.bin", t"third.txt")
      ```

      The flags are `CreateFlag`s (`Replace` to overwrite an existing file, which otherwise
      raises `Tar.Error` with reason `AlreadyExists`; `Parents` to create missing
      directories), a `Tar.Flag` to compress the result, and a `LongNameFormat`. Tar permits
      duplicate names, later entries superseding earlier on extraction, so nothing is checked
      at insert. An I/O failure raises `Tar.Error` with reason `CannotWrite`. For an archive
      whose entries are already in hand, writing a `Tarfile` to the path is simpler.
  refer bitumen.tarPathCreatable
  refer bitumen.TarEntryWriter
  refer bitumen.Tarfile
  entry .TarCreatable   the `Creatable` instance behind `path.create[Tar](flags*)`
    detail
        Makes any path type `Abstractable across Paths to Text` `Creatable` in form `Tar`,
        with a `TarBuilder` as the handle, granting read and write. `tarPathCreatable` is
        the given which supplies it. It needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]`.
    entry #Self   the receiver: the path type
    entry #Form   the format selected: `Tar`
    entry #Operand   the flag type: `CreateFlag | Tar.Flag | LongNameFormat`
    entry #Grants   what the handle is granted: `Grant.Read & Grant.Write`
    entry #Result   the handle lent to the block: `TarBuilder`
    entry #create   writes the archive the block authors to the path, atomically
        Sorts the flags, refuses an existing target without `CreateFlag.Replace`, creates
        parents under `CreateFlag.Parents`, and runs the block with a builder in streaming
        mode (no `Tar.Flag`) or accumulating mode (a `Tar.Flag`), then moves the finished
        temporary file onto the target.
  entry #insert   adds an entry: a `Tar.Entry` as given, or a file from a name and any streamable data
      `insert(entry)` writes any `Tar.Entry` — a directory, a link, a file with a fixed mode
      and owner — through at once on an uncompressed target, or queues it on a compressed
      one. `insert(name, data)` makes a `File` entry from anything `Streamable by Data`,
      with the default mode, owner `0` and a zero timestamp, whose body is read lazily from
      the data as it is written: a large source is never held in memory.
  entry #file   authors one entry with a streamed, unknown-length body, written through a `TarEntryWriter`
      `file(name, mode, user, group, mtime)` runs its block with a `TarEntryWriter` whose
      `put` appends a chunk to the entry's body. On an uncompressed target the chunks go
      straight to disk and the header is backpatched with the final size; on a compressed
      target the chunks are buffered and the entry inserted whole when the block returns.
      The block's result is returned.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

      val target: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "streamed.tar"

      target.create[Tar](CreateFlag.Replace): builder ?=>
        builder.file(t"chunks.bin".as[Tar.Ref], mode = UnixMode(ownerExec = true)): entry ?=>
          entry.put(t"first chunk\n".in[Data])
          entry.put(t"second chunk\n".in[Data])
      ```

entry .TarEntryWriter   the writer lent by `TarBuilder#file`: appends chunks to one entry's body
  detail
      A JVM-only class, in `bitumen.jvm`. Inside `builder.file(name): entry ?=> …`, `entry`
      is a `TarEntryWriter`: `#put` appends one chunk and `#write` streams any streamable
      value. It is an exclusive capability confined to its block, so it cannot be retained
      or returned; when the block ends the entry is complete.
  refer bitumen.TarBuilder
  entry #put   appends one chunk of `Data` to the entry's body
  entry #write   streams any `Streamable by Data` value into the entry's body, chunk by chunk
      Needs a `Buffering` in scope; the value is read through its `Streamable` given and
      each chunk `put` as it arrives.

entry .TarOpenable   the `Openable` instance behind `path.open[Tar](flags*)`
  detail
      A JVM-only class, in `bitumen.jvm`, which makes any path type `Abstractable across
      Paths to Text` `Openable` in form `Tar`, lending a `Tar.Handle`. `tarPathOpenable` is
      the given which supplies it. The file is opened with `java.io` and held open for the
      block; entries parse lazily straight off the live stream, payloads streaming in
      bounded chunks as they are read and draining when a later entry is forced. Archives
      open read-only: a mode including `Write` is refused with `Tar.Error`, reason
      `WriteUnsupported`. The flags are `Tar.Flag`s, naming the compression. It needs a
      `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
  refer bitumen.tarPathOpenable
  refer bitumen.Tar
  entry #Self   the receiver: the path type
  entry #Form   the format selected: `Tar`
  entry #Operand   the flag type: `Tar.Flag`
  entry #Result   the handle lent to the block: `Tar.Handle`
  entry #open   opens the file, runs the block with a `Tar.Handle`, and closes it after

entry .ArOpenable   the `Openable` instance behind `path.open[Ar]()`
  detail
      A JVM-only class, in `bitumen.jvm`, which makes any path type `Abstractable across
      Paths to Text` `Openable` in form `Ar`, lending an `Ar.Handle`. `arPathOpenable` is the
      given which supplies it. The file is opened with `java.io` and held open for the
      block; members parse lazily off the live stream. Archives open read-only: a mode
      including `Write` is refused with `Ar.Error`, reason `WriteUnsupported`. There are no
      flags, so the operand type is `Nothing`. It needs a `Tactic[Ar.Error]` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
  refer bitumen.arPathOpenable
  refer bitumen.Ar
  entry #Self   the receiver: the path type
  entry #Form   the format selected: `Ar`
  entry #Operand   the flag type: `Nothing`, as there are no flags
  entry #Result   the handle lent to the block: `Ar.Handle`
  entry #open   opens the file, runs the block with an `Ar.Handle`, and closes it after

entry .tarPathOpenable   opens a file on disk as a tar archive: `path.open[Tar](flags*)`
    A top-level given in `bitumen.jvm`, for any path type `Abstractable across Paths to
    Text` (a galilei `Path`, or text), supplying a `TarOpenable`. It needs a
    `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` in scope. It is at the top level
    because its subject, the path type, belongs to another library; `import soundness.*`
    brings it in, but a program importing `bitumen.*` must name it, `import
    bitumen.tarPathOpenable`, since a wildcard import does not import givens.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

    val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "release.tar.gz"

    path.open[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip):
      tar.entries.to[List].map:
        case file: Tar.Entry.File => t"${file.entryName} (${file.data.size} bytes)"
        case other                => other.entryName
    ```

entry .arPathOpenable   opens a file on disk as an ar archive: `path.open[Ar]()`
    A top-level given in `bitumen.jvm`, for any path type `Abstractable across Paths to
    Text`, supplying an `ArOpenable`. It needs a `Tactic[Ar.Error]` and a
    `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` in scope. `import soundness.*` brings it in; a program
    importing `bitumen.*` names it, `import bitumen.arPathOpenable`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

    val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "package.deb"

    path.open[Ar]():
      ar.entries.to[List].map(_.name)
    ```

entry .tarPathCreatable   creates a tar archive on disk, authored by a block: `path.create[Tar](flags*)`
    A top-level given in `bitumen.jvm`, for any path type `Abstractable across Paths to
    Text`, supplying a `TarBuilder.TarCreatable`, so that `path.create[Tar](flags*)` lends a
    `TarBuilder` to its block. It needs a `Tactic[Tar.Error]` in scope. `import soundness.*`
    brings it in; a program importing `bitumen.*` names it, `import
    bitumen.tarPathCreatable`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

    val target: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "made.tar.gz"

    target.create[Tar](Tar.Flag.Gzip, CreateFlag.Replace): builder ?=>
      builder.insert(t"data.txt".as[Tar.Ref], t"payload".in[Data])
    ```

entry .extractTo   unpacks a `Tarfile` onto a filesystem, beneath a root directory
    A JVM-only extension on `Tarfile`, in `bitumen.jvm`: the inverse of
    `directory.archive[Tar]()`. Each entry is placed beneath `root` by its `Tar.Ref` — which,
    being relative and free of `..`, cannot land outside it — on any `Posix` plane: a file is
    written with its mode and modification time, a directory is created with its mode, a
    symbolic link is created with its target (replacing an existing link), and a hard link
    is linked to the entry it names. Parent directories which the archive does not list are
    created as needed. A FIFO or device entry raises `Tar.Error` with reason
    `DeviceCreationUnsupported`; a pax or GNU long-name pseudo-entry is skipped.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A directory, archived
      code
          import soundness.*
          import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
          import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
          import filesystemOptions.overwritePreexisting
          import filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks
          import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import systems.javaBaseSystem
          import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

          val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"
          val tarball: Tarfile = source.archive[Tar]()
    frame
      label Compressed and written to disk
      code
          import soundness.*
          import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
          import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
          import filesystemOptions.overwritePreexisting
          import filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks
          import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import systems.javaBaseSystem
          import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

          val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"
          val tarball: Tarfile = source.archive[Tar]()
          val release: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project.tar.gz"
          release.create[File](): handle ?=> handle.write(tarball.gzip)
    frame
      label Unpacked somewhere else
      code
          import soundness.*
          import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
          import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
          import filesystemOptions.overwritePreexisting
          import filesystemOptions.preserveSymlinks
          import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import systems.javaBaseSystem
          import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

          val source: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project"
          val tarball: Tarfile = source.archive[Tar]()
          val release: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "project.tar.gz"
          release.create[File](): handle ?=> handle.write(tarball.gzip)
          val unpacked: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "unpacked"
          tarball.extractTo(unpacked)
    ##
    ```

    It needs `CreateNonexistentParents` and `OverwritePreexisting` policies for the plane
    (imported from `filesystemOptions` by name), a `Tactic[Io.Error]` and a
    `Tactic[Tar.Error]`. Modes are set through the `unix:mode` attribute, and silently left
    as the filesystem's default where that is unsupported. To extract an archive too large
    to hold as a `Tarfile`, iterate `Tarfile.read` or an `open[Tar]` block and build a
    `Tarfile` of one entry at a time.
